msi/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Rust
target/
# Cargo.lock IS COMMITTED, deliberately. The Cargo book's rule is "libraries
# ignore it, binaries commit it", and this workspace ships four executables and
# a Python extension. It was ignored here twice over, and the cost was concrete:
# CI resolved dependencies afresh on every run, so it never built what anyone
# had tested. Measured on 2026-07-30, a clean resolve moved 43 lines against the
# tree the release had been cut from, including a `syn` major version. That also
# made tools/release.ps1's "reproducible artifacts" claim untrue -- a manifest
# recording a commit means nothing if the dependency graph is free to move under
# it. See the second `# Rust` block below for the other copy of this rule.

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local test corpora -- never commit sequence data belonging to a lab.
# Contributors point the test scripts at their own files instead.
corpus/
*.dna
*.ab1
*.scf
*.ztr
!prototype/**/demo-*.dna

# Build/test scratch
*.log
node_modules/
prototype/diag*.js
prototype/dump_gb_counts.js
prototype/js_gb_counts.json
prototype/xcheck_gb.py
prototype/package.json

# Rust
/target/
**/target/
# The duplicate `Cargo.lock` entry lived here. Both copies had to go: leaving
# either one would keep the file ignored, and the first block is where the
# reasoning is written down.

# Build output: the single-file browser tool is generated by tools/build-web.ps1
# from the template plus the compiled wasm. It is ~257 KB of base64 that changes
# on every rebuild, which does not belong in history. Ship it in releases.
prototype/dna-reader.html

# Fetched source archives for the features build. Regenerate with
# `python features/build/build.py --refresh`; the sha256 of each is recorded in
# features/provenance.tsv, which is what actually needs to be reviewable.
features/build/.cache/
packages/circular-map/node_modules/
packages/circular-map/dist/

# Release artifacts. Built by tools/release.ps1 on demand; the manifest records
# the commit they came from, so there is nothing here worth versioning.
/dist/

# The pLannotate taint gate (features/SOURCING.md section 0.4) fetches
# snapgene.csv transiently, compares, and deletes it. That file carries no
# licence of any kind and must never enter this history -- so it is ignored by
# name here, and tools/hooks/pre-commit additionally rejects any staged blob
# whose sha256 is the pinned one, which catches it under a different filename.
snapgene.csv

# The oracle scratchpad, and a near-miss worth recording rather than quietly
# fixing.
#
# Oracle work happens in scratchpad/: Python virtualenvs, downloaded test-vector
# suites, throwaway comparison scripts. None of it belongs in history.
#
# On 2026-08-05, while proving the release-signing path, the Ed25519 RELEASE
# PRIVATE KEY was written to scratchpad/relkey.pem for roughly thirty seconds so
# that openssl could use it. `git add -A` had been run repeatedly in that same
# session. Nothing was committed only because scratchpad/ happened not to exist
# during any of those runs -- it had been created minutes earlier. That is luck,
# not a safeguard. Hence this line, and hence the gate step that looks for key
# material by CONTENT rather than trusting a path to be right.
/scratchpad/

# Private keys, anywhere, under any name.
#
# The release signing key lives OUTSIDE this repository, in a sibling directory,
# and the only copy any machine other than the maintainer's sees is a GitHub
# Actions secret. Nothing matching these patterns should ever be here. The gate
# fails if one is, but a mistyped path should not be able to stage one either.
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
